WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.231 --> 00:00:05.148
(emphatic folk singing begins)

00:00:20.792 --> 00:00:24.959
(emphatic folk singing continues)

00:01:01.176 --> 00:01:04.926
(emphatic folk singing ends)

00:01:07.120 --> 00:01:07.970
- [Interviewer] All right, Lee.

00:01:07.970 --> 00:01:09.680
Lee, where'd you grow up?

00:01:09.680 --> 00:01:11.230
Where are you from originally?

00:01:11.230 --> 00:01:12.713
- Pinon, Arizona.

00:01:13.940 --> 00:01:15.970
- [Interviewer] And you're Navajo?

00:01:15.970 --> 00:01:16.807
- [Leland] Dine'.

00:01:17.730 --> 00:01:19.540
- [Interviewer] What is Dine'?

00:01:19.540 --> 00:01:21.357
- [Leland] Dine' means, "The People,"

00:01:22.240 --> 00:01:25.800
because Kit Carson gave us that name.

00:01:25.800 --> 00:01:27.140
Navajo.

00:01:27.140 --> 00:01:28.320
It's,

00:01:28.320 --> 00:01:29.440
that means "thieves",

00:01:29.440 --> 00:01:31.114
But so we don't, we don't,

00:01:31.114 --> 00:01:34.530
We get offended about that
when we're called Navajos.

00:01:34.530 --> 00:01:35.363
- [Interviewer] Oh yeah?

00:01:35.363 --> 00:01:36.446
- Yeah.

00:01:36.446 --> 00:01:37.930
(Interviewer and Leland
talking simultaneously)

00:01:37.930 --> 00:01:38.807
- [Interviewer] It's a derogatory term,

00:01:38.807 --> 00:01:40.410
for your people.

00:01:40.410 --> 00:01:41.243
- Yeah.

00:01:41.243 --> 00:01:42.163
- [Interviewer] Interesting.

00:01:43.115 --> 00:01:44.773
Tell me about your childhood.

00:01:44.773 --> 00:01:46.150
You had both your parents as a kid?

00:01:46.150 --> 00:01:48.313
- Yeah, there was 13 of us.

00:01:50.600 --> 00:01:52.903
My dad was always an alcoholic,

00:01:54.415 --> 00:01:57.010
and my mom, she was a

00:01:58.560 --> 00:02:02.623
she stay home, she was
a true mother to us.

00:02:04.356 --> 00:02:06.970
And Dale passed away though.

00:02:06.970 --> 00:02:07.865
My grandpa Whitewater, his name is

00:02:07.865 --> 00:02:10.294
(Speaking foreign language).

00:02:10.294 --> 00:02:12.273
(Speaking foreign
language) means Whitewater.

00:02:13.165 --> 00:02:18.165
So we were raised by him
and my, my grandma too.

00:02:19.980 --> 00:02:22.145
You know, her name was Cutfinger.

00:02:22.145 --> 00:02:23.570
(speaking foreign language)

00:02:23.570 --> 00:02:24.748
That's what it means.

00:02:24.748 --> 00:02:25.971
(speaking same word)

00:02:25.971 --> 00:02:26.900
Cutfinger.

00:02:26.900 --> 00:02:28.365
- [Interviewer] How would you,

00:02:28.365 --> 00:02:30.265
how would you describe your childhood?

00:02:32.130 --> 00:02:33.462
- I would like,

00:02:33.462 --> 00:02:35.810
I was sent, sent

00:02:35.810 --> 00:02:38.193
to boarding school when
I was seven years old.

00:02:40.210 --> 00:02:41.165
Seven years old.

00:02:41.165 --> 00:02:43.170
They put me in boarding school,

00:02:43.170 --> 00:02:45.803
because my mom couldn't afford to feed us.

00:02:47.254 --> 00:02:49.513
And we were barely making it by, I guess.

00:02:51.501 --> 00:02:53.380
And of course there
was 13 of us, you know,

00:02:53.380 --> 00:02:56.653
and she sent us to boarding school.

00:02:58.110 --> 00:02:59.603
In Tindley, Arizona.

00:03:02.188 --> 00:03:04.397
That's where I mostly,

00:03:06.382 --> 00:03:09.500
like I got traumatized,

00:03:09.500 --> 00:03:12.010
mentally and physically in my mind,

00:03:12.010 --> 00:03:13.000
to where that,

00:03:13.000 --> 00:03:14.200
how they treated us there.

00:03:14.200 --> 00:03:16.103
- [Interviewer] what kind
of stuff would go on?

00:03:18.390 --> 00:03:20.783
- Of course molestation, of course,

00:03:23.050 --> 00:03:27.350
just beat the shit out of us every day,

00:03:27.350 --> 00:03:29.370
every morning, you know?

00:03:29.370 --> 00:03:31.750
Sort of like a military
style kind of shit,

00:03:31.750 --> 00:03:32.583
you know?

00:03:33.700 --> 00:03:36.080
- [Interviewer] And this
was a foster home run

00:03:36.080 --> 00:03:37.390
by --

00:03:37.390 --> 00:03:39.000
- It's a Tindley boarding school.

00:03:39.000 --> 00:03:39.860
- [Interviewer] A boarding school.

00:03:39.860 --> 00:03:41.750
- It was run by the government.

00:03:41.750 --> 00:03:43.040
- [Interviewer] It was run by

00:03:44.070 --> 00:03:46.780
Caucasians or Indians or...

00:03:46.780 --> 00:03:47.660
- No.

00:03:47.660 --> 00:03:49.180
Oh, by white people.

00:03:49.180 --> 00:03:50.013
- [Interviewer] All right.

00:03:52.180 --> 00:03:55.833
- And from there I tried to move up to,

00:03:58.270 --> 00:04:00.343
I wanted to move on, you know, so,

00:04:01.320 --> 00:04:02.680
and then I went home,

00:04:02.680 --> 00:04:04.193
and then my mom was there,

00:04:05.070 --> 00:04:07.800
and she put me on placement

00:04:09.860 --> 00:04:10.693
with

00:04:12.011 --> 00:04:13.113
LDS.

00:04:15.120 --> 00:04:17.450
Missionaries came around to our home

00:04:17.450 --> 00:04:21.800
to where that they were
sort of like recruiting,

00:04:21.800 --> 00:04:23.630
or whatever the heck you would call it.

00:04:23.630 --> 00:04:28.313
You know, they wanted some people to,

00:04:31.230 --> 00:04:33.390
you know, little kids school,

00:04:33.390 --> 00:04:35.800
to go to school on placement.

00:04:35.800 --> 00:04:37.413
placement program.

00:04:38.830 --> 00:04:39.663
LDS.

00:04:42.130 --> 00:04:44.740
Anyway, I went to school down in Morenci,

00:04:44.740 --> 00:04:46.300
Pima, Fort Thomas,

00:04:46.300 --> 00:04:48.830
and Thatcher way down

00:04:48.830 --> 00:04:50.490
in the Southern area of the state

00:04:51.420 --> 00:04:52.253
in Arizona.

00:04:53.860 --> 00:04:54.758
- After I came back,

00:04:54.758 --> 00:04:56.432
(clearing throat)

00:04:56.432 --> 00:04:58.540
after I came back,

00:04:58.540 --> 00:05:01.823
I decided to do something with my life.

00:05:06.330 --> 00:05:11.200
So I just, you know, voluntarily went

00:05:14.030 --> 00:05:15.593
into military.

00:05:19.170 --> 00:05:20.003
Did that.

00:05:22.550 --> 00:05:24.500
This is a sort of just like, like, like

00:05:25.840 --> 00:05:26.673
Really,

00:05:29.860 --> 00:05:31.963
I still have nightmares about that though.

00:05:33.616 --> 00:05:34.972
- [Interviewer] And that was Afghanistan?

00:05:34.972 --> 00:05:35.805
- Mmhm.

00:05:36.760 --> 00:05:37.593
Yep.

00:05:40.470 --> 00:05:42.307
Until I got out of there,

00:05:45.247 --> 00:05:47.373
And my life hasn't been the same.

00:05:48.800 --> 00:05:49.633
Then I

00:05:51.750 --> 00:05:54.370
lost my mom, lost everybody.

00:05:54.370 --> 00:05:55.743
even my son, you know?

00:05:58.750 --> 00:06:01.393
So I just, and then I'm on the streets.

00:06:02.430 --> 00:06:04.103
- [Interviewer] You been on
the streets for how long?

00:06:05.370 --> 00:06:07.523
- Let's say for about three years now.

00:06:09.490 --> 00:06:11.170
- [Interviewer] Your son was grown?

00:06:11.170 --> 00:06:14.393
Yeah, he was 37 when he passed.

00:06:15.930 --> 00:06:17.330
He passed away.

00:06:17.330 --> 00:06:18.553
- [Interviewer] How did he die?

00:06:19.730 --> 00:06:20.563
- I don't know.

00:06:20.563 --> 00:06:21.650
What they say is, you know,

00:06:21.650 --> 00:06:22.893
is it heart failure?

00:06:25.140 --> 00:06:26.083
Heart failure.

00:06:29.500 --> 00:06:30.793
He died in Minnesota.

00:06:31.790 --> 00:06:35.610
He was an iron worker, very --

00:06:35.610 --> 00:06:36.636
- [Interviewer] Iron worker?

00:06:36.636 --> 00:06:38.300
- Mmhm.

00:06:38.300 --> 00:06:40.430
Like those tall buildings, like, you know,

00:06:40.430 --> 00:06:42.283
stories and stories high.

00:06:44.294 --> 00:06:46.800
And he was a good young kid to me.

00:06:46.800 --> 00:06:47.943
I miss him a lot.

00:06:50.060 --> 00:06:50.893
Every day.

00:06:52.040 --> 00:06:53.583
24 seven, I would say.

00:06:55.610 --> 00:06:56.790
I miss him a lot.

00:06:56.790 --> 00:06:57.623
- [Interviewer] Yeah.

00:06:58.739 --> 00:07:02.143
So alcohol is, is your,
is your, is your drug now?

00:07:04.720 --> 00:07:08.230
- [Leland] I don't do any,
any other than alcohol.

00:07:08.230 --> 00:07:12.163
Like drugs, meth, or you
know, marijuana or anything.

00:07:13.250 --> 00:07:15.355
I just think it depends

00:07:15.355 --> 00:07:18.023
(indistinct)

00:07:18.023 --> 00:07:23.023
to keep occupied in my mind to where that,

00:07:27.670 --> 00:07:30.583
Once it wears off, I started thinking.

00:07:32.080 --> 00:07:32.913
I just

00:07:37.890 --> 00:07:38.923
get emotional.

00:07:40.060 --> 00:07:40.913
That's what I do.

00:07:42.390 --> 00:07:45.323
But alcohol is, it helps me.

00:07:47.060 --> 00:07:49.233
I don't know how that is, but it helps me.

00:07:50.663 --> 00:07:51.709
- [Interviewer] What did, what did

00:07:51.709 --> 00:07:54.033
what, what drink do you go to usually?

00:07:54.870 --> 00:07:55.770
Vodka or --

00:07:55.770 --> 00:07:56.603
- [Leland] Vodka.

00:07:56.603 --> 00:07:58.012
- [Interviewer] Right.

00:07:58.012 --> 00:07:58.845
- [Leland] Yeah. Imported.

00:07:58.845 --> 00:07:59.678
- [Interviewer] Vodka seems popular

00:07:59.678 --> 00:08:01.010
with the Indian population here.

00:08:02.013 --> 00:08:06.000
- [Leland] Yeah. And then this, it's this,

00:08:06.000 --> 00:08:07.820
you know, it makes me happy,

00:08:07.820 --> 00:08:11.083
And then I, I actually out on the streets,

00:08:12.380 --> 00:08:15.630
I can relate to what

00:08:17.320 --> 00:08:18.940
people feel.

00:08:18.940 --> 00:08:20.520
All these people are hurt

00:08:20.520 --> 00:08:21.703
out in the streets.

00:08:22.690 --> 00:08:25.140
They've been hurt one way
or the other, you know,

00:08:26.260 --> 00:08:27.580
one way or another.

00:08:27.580 --> 00:08:30.530
And we, we get along with each other.

00:08:30.530 --> 00:08:34.810
We, we let, you know,

00:08:34.810 --> 00:08:36.810
we back each other up, you know,

00:08:36.810 --> 00:08:38.291
we support each other.

00:08:38.291 --> 00:08:40.166
(Interviewer and Leland
talking simultaneously)

00:08:40.166 --> 00:08:40.999
- [Interviewer] You have
friends on the street.

00:08:40.999 --> 00:08:42.040
- Yeah.

00:08:42.040 --> 00:08:43.670
- This, this, this, this, this, all it is.

00:08:43.670 --> 00:08:44.653
Street people.

00:08:45.550 --> 00:08:46.383
Homeless.

00:08:47.970 --> 00:08:51.090
And we know what we want.

00:08:51.090 --> 00:08:55.490
You know, just keep drinking the alcohol.

00:08:55.490 --> 00:08:56.323
It's just

00:08:57.680 --> 00:08:59.591
- [Interviewer] Native
Americans seem to have a

00:08:59.591 --> 00:09:01.860
susceptibility to,

00:09:01.860 --> 00:09:03.210
to alcoholism it seem, right?

00:09:03.210 --> 00:09:04.043
It's something genetic.

00:09:04.043 --> 00:09:06.450
Isn't it?

00:09:06.450 --> 00:09:07.760
- It would be.

00:09:07.760 --> 00:09:11.880
it is, it has run in my family.

00:09:11.880 --> 00:09:12.713
- [Interviewer] Your dad.

00:09:12.713 --> 00:09:16.490
- Yeah, my dad, my grandpa.

00:09:19.190 --> 00:09:21.990
I mean, my grandpa used to make a run

00:09:21.990 --> 00:09:23.390
on a horseback all the way

00:09:23.390 --> 00:09:25.506
from pinon into Winslow,

00:09:25.506 --> 00:09:27.840
just to get drinks, you know?

00:09:27.840 --> 00:09:29.870
And then he used to come home

00:09:29.870 --> 00:09:30.703
with it,

00:09:30.703 --> 00:09:32.233
and then he used to sell.

00:09:33.200 --> 00:09:37.593
And I remember those,
back in my younger days.

00:09:38.530 --> 00:09:39.930
That's what they used to do.

00:09:41.739 --> 00:09:44.493
And this is, like,

00:09:45.430 --> 00:09:47.363
our lives ain't easy.

00:09:51.490 --> 00:09:52.893
If you come down to,

00:09:54.420 --> 00:09:55.813
if you come down to,

00:09:57.760 --> 00:10:02.760
why, all the treaties been broken.

00:10:04.010 --> 00:10:07.691
1868 treaties, you know about that?

00:10:07.691 --> 00:10:08.524
- [Interviewer] I don't.

00:10:08.524 --> 00:10:09.357
- You don't?

00:10:12.727 --> 00:10:15.073
They've been broken.

00:10:17.020 --> 00:10:19.690
And all the laws that's going on now

00:10:19.690 --> 00:10:20.950
to where that,

00:10:21.940 --> 00:10:25.613
to my understanding to, like, okay.

00:10:28.080 --> 00:10:29.603
You, you, you,

00:10:31.440 --> 00:10:35.500
the history books, science, mathematical,

00:10:35.500 --> 00:10:36.910
and all this history.

00:10:36.910 --> 00:10:39.093
Okay? Social studies and all that?

00:10:40.810 --> 00:10:42.330
How come they don't,

00:10:42.330 --> 00:10:45.130
they don't have it written down there

00:10:45.130 --> 00:10:46.480
in the book to where

00:10:46.480 --> 00:10:49.313
that they don't teach what they did to us.

00:10:52.480 --> 00:10:53.640
How come they don't have that?

00:10:53.640 --> 00:10:55.190
How come they don't teach that?

00:10:56.180 --> 00:10:59.230
Like cutting, the long walk

00:11:00.760 --> 00:11:02.820
to Fort Sumner that they did to us,

00:11:02.820 --> 00:11:07.820
Kit Carson, he cut off
breasts of young ladies.

00:11:09.920 --> 00:11:11.313
He, he killed young ladies.

00:11:18.040 --> 00:11:19.840
How come that's not in history?

00:11:19.840 --> 00:11:23.480
How come they don't teach that in history?

00:11:23.480 --> 00:11:25.880
How come that's not
something in social studies?

00:11:27.250 --> 00:11:29.393
Why is it that they don't bring that up?

00:11:33.010 --> 00:11:33.843
Now.

00:11:35.300 --> 00:11:36.267
All of these,

00:11:36.267 --> 00:11:37.470
(clears throat)

00:11:37.470 --> 00:11:41.093
minerals and environmental
issues that they have now.

00:11:42.890 --> 00:11:46.923
I was in Standing Rock and Cannon Ball.

00:11:51.460 --> 00:11:52.523
To perk this,

00:11:52.523 --> 00:11:54.687
(speaking foreign language)

00:11:54.687 --> 00:11:55.967
"Water is life."

00:12:01.450 --> 00:12:03.280
I stand against that, I'll give you,

00:12:03.280 --> 00:12:04.220
all your reading

00:12:04.220 --> 00:12:06.733
and all these drillings
that are going on now,

00:12:09.010 --> 00:12:10.863
they're just contaminating us.

00:12:11.700 --> 00:12:13.440
We can't even have a spring water.

00:12:13.440 --> 00:12:16.083
They're contaminating all that.

00:12:18.430 --> 00:12:19.750
We have no right, no more.

00:12:19.750 --> 00:12:23.446
Even our plants, even our
corn, even older cornfields.

00:12:23.446 --> 00:12:26.313
We can't grow anything anymore,

00:12:27.830 --> 00:12:30.243
because of the kind of contamination.

00:12:31.850 --> 00:12:33.283
They have coal mines.

00:12:37.837 --> 00:12:40.193
They have all this thing going on.

00:12:43.230 --> 00:12:45.093
So there you have my story.

00:12:46.800 --> 00:12:49.100
- [Interviewer] You feel like
the American Indian has been

00:12:49.100 --> 00:12:52.430
shortchanged by the white man, don't you?

00:12:52.430 --> 00:12:54.976
I mean, it's pretty clear
that it's been going on

00:12:54.976 --> 00:12:56.290
for many years, right?

00:12:56.290 --> 00:12:57.123
Forever.

00:13:00.470 --> 00:13:02.840
Are you, what is the, what
is the religion that Nava--

00:13:02.840 --> 00:13:04.660
that your, that your people follow?

00:13:04.660 --> 00:13:06.444
Is it, it's not Christianity.

00:13:06.444 --> 00:13:09.163
- We don't, we don't have, well,

00:13:09.163 --> 00:13:11.400
- [Interviewer] Are you a religious man?

00:13:11.400 --> 00:13:12.933
- Yeah. I'm tradition.

00:13:14.950 --> 00:13:17.483
We don't believe in
Christianity or, you know,

00:13:18.350 --> 00:13:20.683
we were put through three schools,

00:13:20.683 --> 00:13:23.250
but they tried it and
they very influenced us

00:13:23.250 --> 00:13:27.180
to where that they wanted
us believe in that.

00:13:27.180 --> 00:13:29.005
But we always come back

00:13:29.005 --> 00:13:32.870
to own traditional ways of life, you know?

00:13:32.870 --> 00:13:35.509
- [Interviewer] So what do
you, what do you, what are the,

00:13:35.509 --> 00:13:36.410
what do you believe?

00:13:36.410 --> 00:13:37.723
I mean, do you believe in a God?

00:13:38.970 --> 00:13:40.660
- We don't have a God.

00:13:41.593 --> 00:13:43.463
I don't even know who God is.

00:13:46.250 --> 00:13:49.530
or Jesus or Mary or all that.

00:13:49.530 --> 00:13:50.790
We have our own,

00:13:50.790 --> 00:13:51.734
They call it

00:13:51.734 --> 00:13:54.270
(Speaking foreign language)

00:13:54.270 --> 00:13:56.133
That's, that's, that's our belief.

00:13:58.750 --> 00:14:00.567
What it means is,

00:14:00.567 --> 00:14:03.660
"it's you who has to believe

00:14:05.680 --> 00:14:06.637
in yourself."

00:14:09.550 --> 00:14:12.393
Don't depend on anybody
else, but except yourself.

00:14:13.400 --> 00:14:18.400
Your belief is this corn
pollen, white corn, yellow corn.

00:14:20.510 --> 00:14:22.360
Then we offer that in the morning

00:14:23.660 --> 00:14:25.770
to live another day.

00:14:25.770 --> 00:14:28.140
Thankful for another day.

00:14:28.140 --> 00:14:30.693
Thankful for that we live still.

00:14:35.020 --> 00:14:36.430
- [Interviewer] Is there racism

00:14:36.430 --> 00:14:39.240
against the Native Americans here

00:14:39.240 --> 00:14:40.800
in New Mexico from, from

00:14:40.800 --> 00:14:41.720
- of course.

00:14:41.720 --> 00:14:42.553
- [Interviewer] it's bad, isn't it?

00:14:42.553 --> 00:14:43.453
- Every border.

00:14:46.210 --> 00:14:48.260
Just recently I heard there was another

00:14:48.260 --> 00:14:50.333
incident that happened in Farmington.

00:14:51.180 --> 00:14:52.883
They're real racists over there.

00:14:53.940 --> 00:14:57.910
They just, you know,
beat up native peoples

00:14:57.910 --> 00:14:59.847
out in the streets, you know?

00:15:01.720 --> 00:15:04.273
in Flagstaff, Page,

00:15:06.330 --> 00:15:08.520
Winslow, Holbrook, here

00:15:08.520 --> 00:15:09.523
in Gallup too.

00:15:11.280 --> 00:15:13.693
Very, very racist people.

00:15:15.590 --> 00:15:18.970
But we try to stick up for, you know,

00:15:18.970 --> 00:15:22.293
for each other and try
to save her, you know?

00:15:23.140 --> 00:15:26.903
Try to save ourselves
for all, from all this.

00:15:32.950 --> 00:15:35.040
- [Interviewer] in surviving
out on the streets here

00:15:35.040 --> 00:15:35.873
in New Mexico,

00:15:35.873 --> 00:15:37.493
